{"resourceIdentifier":"4429","resourceName":"Management of Community Forests for the Reduction of Deforestation in the Peruvian Amazon","description":"The REDD + project is implemented in the area made up of 21 native communities: Junín Pablo, Nuevo Loreto, Buenos Aires, Caimito, Nuevo Egipto, Nueva Yarina, Santa Rosa de Dinamarca, Nuevo Paraiso, Santa Marta, Utucuro, Caco Macaya, San José of Pacache, Nuevo Saposoa, Santa Rosa de Aguaytía, Mariscal Cáceres, Yamino, Sinchi Roca II, El Naranajal, Nueva Alianza de Baños, Santa Teresa and Golondrinas, the first 17 communities are located in the Ucayali region and the remaining 4 in the Huánuco region. The project area has an extension of 184,773.74 hectares and its objective is to conserve the forest in the 21 communities from the advance of deforestation and degradation, as well as to conserve biodiversity stocks and improve the living conditions of the population surrounding the project area. .\r\n\r\nThe project aims to reduce the pressure to change land use in the project area through 4 components: i) Strengthening of governance and management of the territory in native communities, ii) Generation of sustainable inclusive businesses, improving income of the families of the native communities, iii) Maintenance of forest conservation objects, and iv) Strengthening of capacities for the MBC with social inclusion and gender equity.\r\n\r\nWith these actions, the project expects to avoid a total of 10,394,747.73 tCO2-e according to the base scenario projected for the first 10 years, with an annual average of net emissions of 1,039,474.77 tCO2-e. In addition, the project includes benefits for the populations involved, as well as for the conservation of biodiversity, where it seeks to contribute to the conservation of forests with high levels of biodiversity with the presence of endemic species, species in danger of extinction or with some degree of extinction. of threat, beyond the benefits of reducing GHG emissions.","lo